$x = 5;
$f = function () use ($x) { return $x; };
$x = 7;
echo $f();

The `use` keyword in a closure captures the variable by value at the time the closure is created. So when `$f` is defined, `$x` is 5, and that value is captured.

Later, `$x` is changed to 7, but the closure still has the captured value of 5.

So `echo $f();` will output `5`.

The question asks whether the instanceof operator in PHP only checks class inheritance and does not consider interfaces.

This is incorrect. In PHP, instanceof checks both class inheritance AND interfaces. If a class implements an interface, then an object of that class will return true when checked against that interface with instanceof.

Example:
```php
interface Greetable {}
class Person implements Greetable {}
$p = new Person();
echo $p instanceof Greetable; // true
```

So the statement is "неверно" (incorrect).

